all three are package then why we call it mcp server?

Good question — this gets at a naming/terminology overlap that's genuinely
worth clarifying.

They're two true things at the same time — not a contradiction:

+-----------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| Perspective                       | What it's called                                                         |
+-----------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| How it's                          | An npm package (just like any other npm package                          |
| distributed/installed             | — same registry, same install mechanism)                                 |
+-----------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| What it DOES when you             | An MCP server (a running program that speaks the MCP protocol)           |
| run it                            |                                                                          |
+-----------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+


Analogy for your background:

Think of nginx:

• How it's distributed:
  it's a .deb /.rpm package (or Docker image)
  — installed via apt / yum / docker pull

• What it does when running:
  it's a web server
  — that's its function, not its packaging format
